    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i get the historical stuff, but that's just the surface level reason. history is always written to explain away things that people don't want to admit are intentional. if you look at the way the streets deviate from the standard grid near the river, it's not just \"pragmatic land division.\" the angles are too specific. look at the convergence points around the intersection of W Adams St and S LaSalle St. if you overlay a geometric pattern there, it feels like a focal point. can you just look at the geometry of those specific intersections for me? ignore the tax records for a second and just tell me if the spatial alignment of those street vectors suggests a centralized node.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
